        "f": "<b>The two formulas</b><br>PV<sub>0</sub> = C<sub>n</sub> / (1 + r)<sup>n</sup><br>NPV = CF<sub>0</sub> + CF<sub>1</sub>/(1+r) + CF<sub>2</sub>/(1+r)<sup>2</sup> + … + CF<sub>N</sub>/(1+r)<sup>N</sup><br><br><b>What r is.</b> A discount rate is a price: it says what one unit of money at date t is worth in money at date t+1, and it is set by what investors could earn elsewhere on an <b>equally risky</b> claim. Everything else is bookkeeping around that price.<br><br><b>Rule 1 — same date, or no addition.</b> Amounts dated at the same period can be added; amounts at different periods cannot. Move them to a common date first.<br><b>Rule 2 — every present value is itself dated.</b> Whatever a PV formula returns sits on a specific date, and that date is not always 0.<br><br>Almost every error in this course breaks one of those two rules rather than the algebra.",
