        "f": "<b>Firm-specific risk</b> (idiosyncratic, unique, diversifiable): events affecting one firm alone — a failed trial, a fire, a lawsuit, a founder resigning. Unrelated across firms.<br><b>Systematic risk</b> (market risk, undiversifiable): events hitting the whole economy — a recession, an oil shock, a shift in the general level of rates. Every firm is exposed, though not equally.<br><br><b>What diversification does.</b> In an equally weighted portfolio of n stocks:<br>Var(R<sub>P</sub>) = (1/n)(average variance) + (1 − 1/n)(average covariance)<br><br>The first term is the firm-specific part and it dies at rate 1/n. The second converges to the average covariance, a positive number, and no amount of diversification touches it.<br><br>If every stock has volatility σ and every pair correlation ρ:<br>σ<sub>P</sub> = σ√(1/n + (1 − 1/n)ρ), with a floor of <b>σ√ρ</b>.",
