        "f": "<b>β<sub>i</sub> = Cov(R<sub>i</sub>, R<sub>mkt</sub>) / Var(R<sub>mkt</sub>) = Corr(R<sub>i</sub>, R<sub>mkt</sub>) × σ<sub>i</sub> / σ<sub>mkt</sub></b><br><br>Beta measures how much a stock <b>amplifies or damps the market's movements</b>. The second form is the one to internalise: beta is <b>correlation scaled by relative volatility</b>, so high volatility raises beta only to the extent that it comes with correlation.<table class=\"data\"><tr><th>β</th><th>Reading</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Moves one for one with the market on average</td></tr><tr><td>1.8</td><td>A 1% market move is associated with a 1.8% move in the stock</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td>No systematic exposure; earns r<sub>f</sub> however wild its own returns are</td></tr><tr><td>negative</td><td>Pays off precisely when everything else does not — valuable, and priced below r<sub>f</sub></td></tr></table>Beta is a <b>covariance</b>, not a volatility. That distinction is examined directly.",
