        "f": "<b>β<sub>i</sub> = (r<sub>i</sub> − r<sub>f</sub>) / (E[R<sub>mkt</sub>] − r<sub>f</sub>)</b><br><br>The excess return the asset offers, divided by the excess return the market offers. <b>Not printed separately on the exam sheet</b>, but the CAPM is, so rearranging is expected. It works on equity (r<sub>E</sub> gives β<sub>E</sub>), on debt (r<sub>D</sub> gives β<sub>D</sub>) and on the unlevered firm (r<sub>U</sub> gives β<sub>U</sub>).<br><br><b>β<sub>P</sub> = w<sub>1</sub>β<sub>1</sub> + w<sub>2</sub>β<sub>2</sub> + … + w<sub>n</sub>β<sub>n</sub></b>, with <b>market-value</b> weights.<br><br><b>Betas add up linearly; volatilities do not.</b> This one property is what makes the whole unlever/relever machinery possible: a firm is a portfolio of its debt and its equity, so its asset beta is the value-weighted average of β<sub>D</sub> and β<sub>E</sub>.<br><br><b>Consequence worth stating:</b> weighting betas and then applying the CAPM gives the same answer as applying the CAPM and then weighting returns.",
