        "f": "<b>r<sub>E</sub></b> — the return equity holders require, given the risk of the residual claim <i>after</i> debt is served.<br><b>r<sub>D</sub></b> — the return debt holders <b>expect</b> to earn on average.<br><b>r<sub>U</sub></b> — the return the assets themselves must earn, a property of the <i>business</i>, not of the financing.<br><br><b>Weights are always market values.</b> If the exam gives you a share price and a share count, multiply them: that is E. Book equity from a balance sheet is the wrong number and propagates into every later step. Debt is usually close enough to book that the exam lets you use it, but say so explicitly if you rely on it.<br><br><b>r<sub>D</sub> is not the promised yield y.</b> The yield is what the bond pays if nothing goes wrong; r<sub>D</sub> is the average across all states, and r<sub>D</sub> = y − p·L is below y whenever default is possible. Putting y into the WACC overstates it.",
