        "f": "<b>Zero-coupon:</b> P = F/(1 + y)<sup>n</sup> &nbsp;⟹&nbsp; <b>y = (F/P)<sup>1/n</sup> − 1</b>. For n = 1 this is just y = F/P − 1.<br><br><b>Coupon bond:</b> P = C·[1 − (1 + y)<sup>−n</sup>]/y + F/(1 + y)<sup>n</sup> — an annuity of coupons plus the face value. y is defined implicitly and must be found by iteration or interpolation when n exceeds 2.<br><br><b>Coupon rate versus yield.</b> The <b>coupon rate</b> is a contract term: it fixes the krone amount the borrower must pay, and it never changes. The <b>yield</b> is a market outcome: whatever discount rate reconciles today's price with those fixed payments.<table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Relation</th><th>Bond trades</th></tr><tr><td>coupon rate below yield</td><td>at a <b>discount</b> (P below F)</td></tr><tr><td>coupon rate equals yield</td><td>at <b>par</b></td></tr><tr><td>coupon rate above yield</td><td>at a <b>premium</b></td></tr></table>y is the <b>promised</b> return: what you earn if and only if every promised krone arrives. Price and yield always move in opposite directions.",
