        "n": 2,
        "k": "Payout in perfect markets",
        "s": "Explain why homemade dividends defeat every payout argument in a perfect market, and state the boundary of the result.",
        "f": "<b>The firm has exactly one financial capability its shareholders lack: none.</b><br><br>Whatever pattern of cash the payout policy delivers, a shareholder can <b>undo</b> it — by selling a slice of his holding, buying more shares, borrowing, or lending, all at the same prices the firm faces. A service you can perform for yourself at no cost has a market value of <b>zero</b>, so no payout policy can be worth paying for.<table class=\"data\"><tr><th>The firm does</th><th>The shareholder who wanted the opposite</th></tr><tr><td>Repurchases instead of paying a dividend</td><td>Sells the shares he wanted paid out — a <b>homemade dividend</b></td></tr><tr><td>Pays a dividend he did not want</td><td>Reinvests it at the <b>ex-dividend</b> price</td></tr><tr><td>Defers the payout by a year</td><td>Borrows the present value today and repays out of the receipt</td></tr><tr><td>Pays out today when he wanted it later</td><td>Lends at the same rate</td></tr></table><b>This is the same argument that killed homemade leverage in k6</b>, applied to the timing and the form of cash rather than to the debt ratio.<br><br><b>The check that makes it rigorous, and the check that fools you.</b> Compare <b>ownership fractions</b>, not share counts, because the two routes leave different numbers of shares outstanding. A shareholder who reinvests a 600 dividend at the ex-price of 32.00 buys 18.75 shares and ends holding 118.75 of 25.000000 million; one who held through a repurchase holds 100 of 21.052632 million. Both are <b>0.00000475</b> of the firm ✓ <b>This can fail:</b> reinvest at the stale cum price of 38.00 and you buy only 15.7895 shares, the fractions no longer agree, and the error is named.<br><br><b>The boundary, which is the whole subject.</b> Irrelevance holds <b>with investment policy fixed</b>. If paying a dividend forces the firm to skip a positive-NPV project, value changes — but it changes because <b>investment</b> changed, not because of the payout. Any argument that a payout created or destroyed value must therefore point to a friction: taxes, transaction costs, asymmetric information, or cash being spent badly inside the firm.",
