        "k": "Payout with frictions",
        "s": "Why does the price fall by less than the dividend when dividends are taxed more heavily than capital gains, and what would happen at the naive full drop?",
        "f": "<b>Because the seller and the holder are not comparing gross amounts; they are comparing what they keep.</b><br><br>A krone of <b>dividend</b> is worth (1 − τ<sub>d</sub>) in the hand. A krone of <b>price</b> given up saves (1 − τ<sub>g</sub>) of taxable gain. If dividends are taxed more heavily, each krone of dividend is worth <b>less</b> than a krone of price, so the price cannot fall by the full amount or nobody would hold through the date:<br>P<sub>cum</sub> − P<sub>ex</sub> = Div × (1 − τ<sub>d</sub>)/(1 − τ<sub>g</sub>)<br>The ratio is the dividend's <b>after-tax exchange rate against a capital gain</b>, and that is the entire content of τ*<sub>d</sub> = (τ<sub>d</sub> − τ<sub>g</sub>)/(1 − τ<sub>g</sub>).<br><br><b>What happens at the naive full drop — the disequilibrium argument.</b> Take P<sub>cum</sub> = 60.00, Div = 4.00, τ<sub>d</sub> = 30%, τ<sub>g</sub> = 20%, basis 40.00. The correct drop is 4.00 × 0.875000 = <b>3.50</b>, so P<sub>ex</sub> = <b>56.50</b>, and both strategies pay <b>56.00</b> after tax ✓<br>Assume the full drop instead: P<sub>ex</sub> = 56.00. Then selling cum pays 60.00 − 0.20(20.00) = <b>56.00</b>, while holding through pays 2.80 + 56.00 − 0.20(16.00) = <b>55.60</b>. <b>Everyone would sell cum-dividend</b>, which is not an equilibrium — so the ex price must be bid up until the two strategies tie.<br><br><b>Note the structure of the penalty.</b> Dividends here are taxed 10 percentage points harder than gains, but the penalty <b>relative to a capital gain</b> is 12.50%, because the gain itself is taxed. That is why τ*<sub>d</sub> divides by (1 − τ<sub>g</sub>) rather than simply differencing the rates.<br><br><b>The practical point about cost basis.</b> It is whatever the question says the investor paid — not the current price and not the ex-dividend price. If no basis is given, <b>state</b> that you assume the shares were bought at the current price so the gain is zero, and compute on that. Stating the assumption earns the mark; inventing a number silently does not.",
