        "n": 2,
        "k": "Payout with frictions",
        "s": "Explain the clientele effect, why it can make the dividend tax penalty invisible in market prices, and why it does not solve the dividend puzzle.",
        "f": "<b>Investors do not share a tax rate</b>, so they do not share an opinion about payout policy.<table class=\"data\"><tr><th>Investor type</th><th>Effective τ*<sub>d</sub></th><th>Prefers</th></tr><tr><td>Tax-exempt: pension funds, endowments, sheltered accounts</td><td>zero</td><td>Indifferent, so happy to hold high-dividend shares nobody else wants</td></tr><tr><td>Taxable individual with τ<sub>d</sub> above τ<sub>g</sub></td><td>positive</td><td>Repurchases and retained gains</td></tr><tr><td>Long-horizon investor who rarely sells</td><td>positive and <b>larger</b>, since deferral is worth more to him</td><td>Repurchases</td></tr><tr><td>Investor needing regular income with costly trading</td><td>positive but outweighed by transaction costs</td><td>Dividends, as a free homemade-dividend service</td></tr></table><b>The sorting.</b> Investors who dislike dividends buy the firms that do not pay them; those who do not care buy the firms that do. Once the sorting is complete, the <b>marginal</b> investor — the one who is indifferent at the current price and therefore sets it — is by construction someone who does not mind the policy. So the price shows <b>no penalty</b>.<br><br><b>What that explains.</b> Why the ex-dividend drop in real data sits closer to the <b>full</b> dividend than a top personal tax rate would predict: the marginal holder over the ex-date is often a tax-exempt institution or a trader with a low effective rate.<br><br><b>What it does not explain, and this is the point of the question.</b> Why the firm <b>chose the policy in the first place</b>. Supplying a clientele that is already well served creates nothing — there are plenty of dividend-paying firms already, so being one more adds no value. The puzzle survives at the aggregate level: why is so much paid out as dividends at all, when the repurchase route is cheaper in tax for the marginal taxable investor?<br><br><b>The answers the course offers, and the ones to name.</b> <b>Signalling</b> (a sticky dividend commits, and the commitment is informative) and the <b>agency argument</b> (cash that has left cannot be wasted). Clienteles explain the <b>cross-section</b>; those two explain the <b>level</b>.",
        "id": 32
